# NO TRAILING SLASH ON node_modules, and that single character is the whole point.
# `node_modules/` is a DIRECTORY-ONLY pattern. Every plan in docs/superpowers/plans ends its tasks
# with `git add -A`, and in a git worktree node_modules is a SYMLINK to the parent checkout — not a
# directory, so the slashed pattern never matched and `git add -A` staged the link itself. The
# committed artifact is a dangling symlink to ../../../node_modules that resolves only from inside
# one specific worktree layout and is broken in every clone. Enforced by a test in
# test/pipeline.test.mjs that asks git's own matcher rather than grepping this file.
node_modules
.wrangler/
dist/
.dev.vars

# NO TRAILING SLASH ON THESE EITHER, for the same reason as node_modules above — and CI proved it on
# its first ever run (2026-07-26). `.superpowers/` is a DIRECTORY-ONLY pattern, so `git check-ignore`
# reports the path as NOT ignored on any checkout where the directory does not happen to exist. It
# passed locally (the directory is there) and failed in CI (a clean clone, where it is not), which is
# precisely the "a pattern that LOOKS right does not MATCH" trap the node_modules note describes.
# Slashless matches whether the path exists, is a directory, or is a symlink.
#
# Agent scratch. Swept in by the same `git add -A` and equally not ours to commit.
.superpowers
# Browser-automation scratch (traces, page snapshots, screenshots) written during live verification.
# Same rule as .superpowers: produced by a tool, not by this project, and `git add -A` would take it.
.playwright-mcp
# Python bytecode from container/. Written by any `python3 -m py_compile container/server.py` — the
# standard way to syntax-check the resolver before a container build — so it appears during ordinary
# verification, not just in unusual sessions. Same `git add -A` hazard as the two patterns above.
__pycache__

# AGENT AND EDITOR STATE THAT MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED, and `.claude` is the one that matters:
# in this workspace `.claude/settings.local.json` is exactly where GitHub tokens live. It has been
# protected only by a MACHINE-GLOBAL ignore file, which does not travel to a clone, a fork, CI, or a
# second machine — so the protection was real here and absent everywhere else.
.claude

# Every spelling of a secrets file, not just the one that happens to exist today. `.dev.vars` alone
# left `.dev.vars.staging`, `.env`, and `container/.env` unignored, which is the same "a pattern that
# LOOKS right does not MATCH" trap the node_modules note above describes.
.env
.env.*
.dev.vars.*

# THE FILES THE ACCOUNT POOLS MAKE SOMEBODY DOWNLOAD, which the patterns above do not cover and
# which did not exist as a risk until X_ACCOUNTS / IG_ACCOUNTS / YT_ACCOUNTS shipped.
#
# The workflow those secrets require is: export a cookies.txt from a browser you are logged into,
# save it, paste its contents into `wrangler secret put`. Every step of that ends with a live session
# sitting in a file, and the obvious place to save it is whatever directory the terminal is already
# in — which is this one. `.dev.vars` never matched any of these names.
#
# A LEAKED JAR IS A LOGGED-IN ACCOUNT, not a config value: it is a bearer credential that works from
# anywhere until the account's session is revoked, and it cannot be rotated by changing a password
# somebody would have to notice was changed.
#
# Slashless and broad on purpose, per the note above: `cookies.txt` at the root and
# `container/cookies.txt` are the same mistake, and the second one is likelier because that is where
# the thing consuming it lives.
cookies.txt
*cookies.txt
*.cookies
cookies*.json
accounts.json
*accounts.json
secrets.json
*.secrets

# NOT ignored, and deliberately: the committed example carries invented values so somebody can see
# the shape without ever opening a real one. If this line ever has to be removed, the example has
# stopped being an example.
!accounts.example.json
